Q3) Checked Exception
Checked exceptions are checked by the compiler at compile time.


**********************************Example:
import java.io.FileReader;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        FileReader f = new FileReader("abc.txt");        //Compiler complains because FileReader 
                                                         //  can throw FileNotFoundException.
    }
}

************************************You need to handle it:
try {
    FileReader f = new FileReader("abc.txt");
}
catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("File not found");
}

*********************Interview answer
Checked exceptions are checked by the compiler at compile time. We must either handle them using try-catch or declare them using throws.

******************************Q: Why are they called checked?
Because the compiler checks whether they are handled or declared.


*************Examples
IOException
SQLException
FileNotFoundException
ClassNotFoundException

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q3) Unchecked Exception

Unchecked exceptions are not checked by the compiler.
They generally occur during runtime.

*********************Example:
int a = 10 / 0;

*********************Output:
ArithmeticException

************Q: Is RuntimeException checked or unchecked?
RuntimeException is an unchecked exception.


